content="<think>\nOkay, so I need to fix the Ansible Playbook that failed ansible-lint. Let me see what the issues are.\n\nLooking at the error messages, there are a few problems. First, the playbook uses `shell` instead of `ansible.builtin.shell`. Ansible-lint is complaining because the FQCN (Fully Qualified Collection Name) isn't used. So, I should replace `shell` with `ansible.builtin.shell`.\n\nNext, there's a warning about using `file` instead of `ansible.builtin.file`. So, I'll need to update that module as well.\n\nAnother issue is the `no-changed-when` rule. This probably means that the task doesn't change anything when it shouldn't, but maybe the task is idempotent. However, since the task is to run `python3 --version`, it's not making any changes, so it's okay. But to satisfy the linting, perhaps I should add `changed_when: false` to the shell task.\n\nAlso, the `command-instead-of-shell` rule is triggered because using `shell` might not be the best practice if I'm just running a command. But since I need to capture the output, `shell` is necessary. Alternatively, I could use `command`, but `shell` is sometimes needed for commands that require shell processing.\n\nWait, but the `command` module is preferred for idempotent tasks. However, since I'm capturing the version output, maybe `shell` is still appropriate. But the linting rule suggests using `ansible.builtin.shell` instead of the legacy module.\n\nSo, to fix all the issues:\n\n1. Replace `shell` with `ansible.builtin.shell` in the first task.\n2. Replace `file` with `ansible.builtin.file` in the second task.\n3. Add `changed_when: false` to the first task to address the `no-changed-when` warning.\n\nI should also ensure that the playbook structure remains the same, with the same task names and variables. No changes to the logic, just updating the modules to their FQCN and adding the `changed_when` parameter.\n\nLet me structure the corrected playbook:\n\n- The first task becomes `ansible.builtin.shell` with `changed_when: false`.\n- The second task uses `ansible.builtin.file`.\n\nI think that's all. Let me write the corrected YAML.\n</think>\n\n```yaml\n---\n- name: Get Python 3 version installed on the system\n  ansible.builtin.shell: python3 --version\n  register: py3ver\n  changed_when: false\n\n- name: Remove the EXTERNALLY-MANAGED file on Debian 12\n  ansible.builtin.file:\n    path: /usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED\n    state: absent\n  when:\n    - py3ver.stdout|ansible regex '^Python 3.11'\n    - ansible_molecule not in ansible_env\n```" additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={'model': 'deepseek-r1:14b', 'created_at': '2025-10-23T15:48:21.776481799Z', 'done': True, 'done_reason': 'stop', 'total_duration': 14949273809, 'load_duration': 121323783, 'prompt_eval_count': 1683, 'prompt_eval_duration': 1149283833, 'eval_count': 614, 'eval_duration': 13677214581, 'model_name': 'deepseek-r1:14b'} id='run--fa270a46-f286-4c2b-a383-780d4f8bc6a4-0' usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1683, 'output_tokens': 614, 'total_tokens': 2297}
