 which is a module, but perhaps the way it\'s structured is causing the issue. But I don\'t think so because \'import_tasks\' is a valid module.\n\nWait, looking at the error message again: "mapping values are not allowed here". That usually happens when you try to put a mapping (key-value pairs) where a scalar is expected. So perhaps in the structure, after \'tasks:\', the list items are not correctly formed.\n\nWait, in the original YAML, the tasks are correctly listed. Maybe the issue is that the \'tasks\' key is not followed by a list but by a block. Let me see: in the original, it\'s written as:\n\ntasks:\n  - name: ...\n    import_tasks: ...\n\nWhich is correct. So why the error? Maybe the problem is that the tasks are not properly indented. Let me check the spaces.\n\nIn YAML, indentation is significant. Each level is typically 2 or 4 spaces. So if the tasks are indented with 2 spaces, but the rest of the document uses 4, that could cause issues. Alternatively, perhaps the list items are not properly aligned.\n\nWait, another thought: The error is at line 3, which is the first task. So maybe the problem is that the \'tasks\' key is not correctly followed by a list. Let me see: the original YAML starts with \'---\', then \'tasks:\', then each task is a list item. That should be correct.\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is that the \'tasks\' key is not followed by a list but by a block of key-value pairs, which isn\'t allowed. Because in Ansible, \'tasks\' should be a list of tasks.\n\nWait, in the original, it\'s written as:\n\ntasks:\n  - name: ...\n    import_tasks: ...\n\nWhich is correct. So maybe the issue is that the \'tasks\' key is not followed by a list, but by a block that\'s not a list. Wait, no, because each task starts with a \'-\'.\n\nWait, perhaps the issue is that the \'tasks\' list is not properly started. Let me see: the playbook starts with \'---\', then \'tasks:\', then the list. That\'s correct.\n\nWait, maybe the problem is that the \'tasks\' are not under a \'play\' or \'hosts\' structure. Because Ansible playbooks typically have a structure like:\n\n- hosts: all\n  tasks:\n    - name: task1\n      module: ...\n\nBut in the original playbook, it\'s just the tasks listed directly under \'--- tasks: ...\', which might be causing the issue because it\'s missing the \'hosts\' or \'play\' structure.\n\nAh, that\'s probably it. The original playbook is missing the \'hosts\' directive. Ansible expects a play to have \'hosts\' specified. So the playbook is not correctly structured as an Ansible playbook.\n\nSo the fix is to wrap the tasks under a \'hosts\' or \'plays\' structure. Let me adjust that.\n\nI\'ll structure it as a single play with \'hosts: all\' and include all the tasks under \'tasks\'. That should fix the YAML structure.\n\nSo the corrected playbook will start with \'---\', then a \'hosts: all\' key, then \'tasks\' as a list of tasks. This way, the YAML is valid and passes yamllint.\n</think>\n\n---\n- hosts: all\n  tasks:\n    - name: Task 1: Import preflight tasks for Promtail installation\n      import_tasks: promtail/preflight.yml\n\n    - name: Task 2: Import tasks for installing Promtail\n      import_tasks: promtail/install.yml\n\n    - name: Task 3: Ensure Promtail service is started and enabled\n      shell: systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl enable promtail && systemctl start promtail' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={'model': 'deepseek-r1:14b', 'created_at': '2025-10-23T16:23:04.732257403Z', 'done': True, 'done_reason': 'stop', 'total_duration': 28295481246, 'load_duration': 80444038, 'prompt_eval_count': 484, 'prompt_eval_duration': 71452017, 'eval_count': 1240, 'eval_duration': 28142451100, 'model_name': 'deepseek-r1:14b'} id='run--9ed8803e-aa0e-42e8-bd68-66cef8b687aa-0' usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 484, 'output_tokens': 1240, 'total_tokens': 1724}
